cult  because of the lack of a real gold  standard  so i think based on the data we've  discussed today uh i think we can say  that if you incorporate uh results of  the universe panel so a multiplex  bacterial pcr of the broncho of vel  lavage into an antibiotic stewardship  approach that should be going on  everywhere anyway  it makes sense it is efficient it's safe  and it really decreases time on  inappropriate antibiotic therapy  particularly if we look at the  population we examine here hospitalized  patients with  pneumonia  at risk for negative roads  and  for sure there are other questions that  have to be addressed in the future  the cost of this  of this approach i believe we will  reduce significantly the costs because  patients will receive less antibiotics  have less side effects and probably go  home earlier but we will need larger  studies to confirm to confirm this this  findings  so  take home messages i would say that the  main advantage of a bacteriomorphic pcr  relies in its  speed i mean it's a fast examination you  have results very early and you were  able to adapt antibiotic therapy very  early  it has a higher sensitivity than  conventional methods  if we talk about the universal if we  look about  specif specifically if we focus on run  negative roads i think we can say that  and  we do not have any  data support in the use of this  technology alone we cannot  refrain from performing conventional  microbiology  and we cannot also not say that we do  not need antibiotic stewardship it's  very important but if we combine  antibiotic stewardship with the pcr  results then we have a clear gain for  our patients we can reduce the the  duration of inappropriate antibiotic  therapy and i think this might have  major consequences for patients and  institutions  so with that i will stop here uh thank  you for your attention and i'll be very  happy to take your questions and discuss  uh how do you see the results how you  can apply this technology to your  clinical work thank you very much  thank you professor stoltz for your  informative presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar if you have a question  you would like to ask please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  we'll answer as many of your questions  as we have time for  let's get started our first question is  which gram negative bras were most  prevalent in your study  yes so thank you for the question and i  think it's an important information that  i didn't share with you uh but i think  the the ones we seen most commonly were  are the ones that we expected  pseudomonas aeruginosa and hemophilus in  frenzy i must say  switzerland especially the north of  switzerland is  blessed by have been very restrictive  with the use of broad spectrum  antibiotics we do not have  highly resistant  negative roads  in our icus so far  so we  we don't see things like we used to say  to see in the u.s with really a lot of  very resistant glue negative bacteria  but we do see the same spectrum of  bacteria in our care here  thank you professor stoltz the next  question is based on the impact that  univero made which were reduction in the  use of inappropriate antibiotic therapy  by 45.1 percent  shortened inappropriate antibiotic  therapy by 39 hours and three times  higher probability of avoiding  inappropriate antibiotic therapy etc  what is the cost savings impact  yeah that's a good question um  the study didn't focus on  the  costs  or the cost savings that this technology  created but i think it's a sensible  question to ask  i believe there are two different  important parts of the information here  the one is how much money we can save  really within one hospitalization by  decreasing the exposure to antibiotics  and probably side effects  the other one is also the impact that it  has in a broader view  meaning you know what happens to the  development of resistance within a  hospital as such  so um i think that's an important  question to explore i cannot offer you  an answer right now but i'm sure um it  makes sense to to go after that and i'm  sure that the benefits won't be only  financial for one particular patient but  we will also transfer into savings for  an institution  thank you professor stoltz i'd like to  remind the audience that if you would  like to ask a question please do so now  click on the ask a question box and then  type in your question click send  we have one more question in the queue  here is the univaro system easy to use  was it run in the lab or was it run by  the medical staff during your study  yeah well we organized the the study in  a way that we had the universe system  located next to the bronchoscopy suite  it's it's quite a small system so you  find place almost everywhere to place it  and it's actually quite easy to use  the test was performed by our endoscopy  staff so immediately after finishing the  bronchovirus lavage and sending the  patient back to the station  they prepared the probe  for testing  and the bronchovirus fluid has been  placed in the machine  it's really quite intuitive and after  pro appropriate training i think  everybody is able to um to deal with it  um i think it has a potential to be a  point of care test  and it saves for short time i think it's  also interesting that we've seen that  for a proportion of patients  physicians were aware that they would  get the result within four hours so they  kind of waited to get the result of the  pcr to start until epic therapy because  they thought okay four hours it's a  time span that it's okay to wait i can  for sure not wait one or two days but  four hours it's okay so we've seen a lot  of patients that start receiving  appropriate antibiotic therapy to begin  with  and i think this time aspect is quite  important  because it really decreases  the exposure to the wrong drugs it's  much more difficult in the hospital to  change a therapy that has been already  established than to  start to begin with with the right  therapy  thank you again professor stoltz looks  like we have no other questions in the  queue do you have any final comments for  our audience before we go  well i think this is an extremely  interesting  field to explore and  i i advise everyone that is involved in  the treatment of patients with severe  pulmonary disease to consider this  technology  as  we really have to care about um  decreasing antibiotic resistance and  making sure that we are treating the  right patient with the right drug um it  has been a major development for our  hospital we do have a lot of  stem cell transplant patients so we're  very happy to count on  a very fast test that is reliable and  help us to treat patients in a better  way  thank you again professor diana stoltz  for your time today and for your  important research we would also like to  thank lab roots and our sponsor opjen  for underwriting today's educational  webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand lab  routes will alert you via email when  it's available for replay we encourage  you to share that email with your  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event  until next time goodbye
 

